in this lesson we're going to talk about  volumes and cylinders so this is an  important concept that they test on the  new revised s80 they seem to emphasize  this quite a bit but they do give you  the formula which makes it a little bit  easier the volume is equal to PI R  squared times the height and remember pi  is 3.14 the radius is the distance from  the center of the circle to appoint on  edge of the circle sometimes they give  you the diameter which is double the  radius and of course the height is you  know how tall is it the distance between  the two bases the two circles so let's  get into some examples go ahead and  pause the video so you can do these on  your own will go through them together  so example one says if a bucket in the  shape of the cylinder right can hold 100  pi cubic inches of water what is the  height of the cylinder in inches if the  diameter is 10 so I always like to draw  a diagram you know if they don't give  you a diagram just to kind of visualize  so it looks something like this the  diameter is 10 which means that the  radius is 5 right and we're trying to  solve for the height so usually in a  problem like this I like that just write  down the formula and then substitute in  what I know and in this case the radius  is 5 and the height we don't know okay  so if we divide both sides by PI those  are going to cancel out 5 squared is  just 5 times 5 which is 25 and if we  divide both sides by 25 to get h by  itself you can see that h is going to be  4 inches so that was a pretty  straightforward problem we're not all  going to be that easy but some of them  are let's go to a more challenging one  with example number two so it says that  John has 12 identical glasses filled  with water okay in the shape of  cylinders with a diameter of 2 inch of  each right and a height of 4 inches each  and he pours them into a cylindrical  bucket right okay with a diameter of 8  inches how high will the bucket be  filled okay that's a good question right  so again let's just draw a picture so  he's got these glasses or like drinking  glasses right and there have a diameter  of 2 inches like so and have a height of  4 inches okay so what's the volume of  each one of these glasses well it's  going to be PI R square times H the  radius is going to be half the diameter  which is 1 so that's going to be pi  times 1 squared times the height of 4 1  squared is 1 so this is just going to be  4 pi right  for one glass but he's got 12 of them so  if we multiply that by twelve you can  see that's going to be 48 pi inches  cubed for the volume but what happens  he's going to pour them into a larger  bucket like this right and larger bucket  has a diameter of 8 inches okay which  means that the radius is only 4 inches  and we're trying to solve for how high  it's going to fill up this bucket right  so basically go back to our volume for a  cylinder PI R squared H and we know that  the volume is 48 pie okay for all those  12 glasses were filled with water the  radius in this larger bucket is 4 inches  that's 4 squared and we're trying to  solve for the height well 4 squared is  16 so this is 16 pi times eh if we  divide both sides by PI those are going  to cancel out and if we divide both  sides by 16 you can see that the height  is going to be 3 inches so that's how  high it's going to reach in the bucket  so again this was just a multiple-step  prom which oftentimes they give you on  the svt to you know see if you can do  that multiple steps and sometimes we're  testing out more than one concepts /  given problem but again it's just you  want to familiarize yourself with the  formula cylinder and review this lesson  if you need to otherwise i'll see you in  the next lesson
 

